```c
// Establish a global rule: always lock mutexA before mutexB, everywhere.

// Thread 1                      // Thread 2 (fixed)
lock(&mutexA);                   lock(&mutexA);   // was mutexB — now matches the rule
lock(&mutexB);                   lock(&mutexB);   // was mutexA
// ... critical section ...      // ... critical section ...
unlock(&mutexB);                 unlock(&mutexB);
unlock(&mutexA);                 unlock(&mutexA);
```

Now, whichever thread gets `mutexA` first, the other simply blocks waiting for `mutexA` too — it never gets partway in by holding `mutexB` first. There's no scenario where each thread holds one lock the other wants, because both threads always request in the same order. The fix required no new synchronization primitive, no rollback logic, and no advance knowledge of resource needs — just a consistent convention.
